lines=['# Shared fast and slow stress: joint VIX experiment','', '27 October 2026. All four maturities fitted together; no expiry-specific parameters.', '',f"The bounded pilot changes checking option loss from **{b['option_loss']:.6f} to {a['option_loss']:.6f}** ({improvement:+.2f}% improvement), with {b['inside_bidask']} → {a['inside_bidask']} of 217 native quotes inside bid–ask. This is a numerical result on the same market snapshot, not evidence across market regimes.",'',f"Training option loss: {tb['option_loss']:.6f} → {t['option_loss']:.6f}. Maximum training forward residual: {max(abs(np.array(t['forward_errors']))):.3g} VIX points. Checking forward residuals are shown below; training equality does not establish population equality.",'','## The model in plain language','', 'The old M2 response describes the original variance mechanism. The stress part can now react on two different timescales: one forgets shocks quickly, while the other keeps them for longer. Every option expiry uses the same states, weights, amplitudes and half-lives. Variance is mixed first; the VIX square root and option payoff come afterwards.','', '$$v_t=f+(\\xi(t)-f)\\left[(1-w)R_t+w\\{(1-r)A_t^s+rA_t^f\\}\\right],$$', '', '$$A_t^i=\\exp(b_i Z_t^i-\\tfrac12b_i^2q_i(t)),\\quad dZ_t^i=-a_iZ_t^i dt+\\sqrt{2a_i}dB_t^i,\\quad a_i=\\log2/h_i.$$', '', 'The two stress Brownian innovations are independent of each other and the old M2 bank. Each normalized response has mean one, so $E_Q[v_t]=\\xi(t)$. The seven active variance levels and the floor are still fitted. See [the full plan](TWO_SPEED_PLAN.md) for normalization, conditional expectations, bounds, and estimator details.', '', 'This tests the simpler endpoint representation motivated by the previous continuous-measure experiment, with an additional stress state. It does not refit the Beta measure at the same time. Three variance branches now replace the old M2-plus-single-stress pair; this is not an arbitrary continuous measure over speeds.', '', '## Fitted shared parameters','', '| Parameter | Fitted value |','|---|---:|',f"| Slow half-life | {m['slow_half_days']:.4f} days |",f"| Fast half-life | {m['fast_half_days']:.4f} days |",f"| Slow amplitude | {m['b_slow']:.6f} |",f"| Fast amplitude | {m['b_fast']:.6f} |",f"| Total stress fraction $w$ | {m['stress_weight']:.6f} |",f"| Fast fraction of stress $r$ | {m['fast_fraction']:.6f} |",f"| Floor volatility | {100*np.sqrt(m['floor_variance']):.4f}% |",'', 'The slow half-life and floor volatility reach their upper bounds, as do two active variance levels; the first active level reaches the floor. Three starts converge to essentially the same best fit. The fourth reaches its iteration limit and misses forward feasibility, so it is excluded and preserved in the optimizer ledger. These results do not establish that the bounds are economically optimal.', '', 'The old M2 smooth cap remains unchanged (level 5.5027800568, sharpness 2). The stress exponentials have no hard state cap; their Gaussian normalization makes their means finite. The amplitude bound of 5 is a calibration parameter bound, not a cap on realized variance.', '', 'The labels fast/slow enforce ordering, not separate rules for short/long options. Either component affects every maturity. Amplitudes are bounded by 5, half-lives by 1–365 days, floor volatility by 5–12%, and free variance levels by floor–0.2. Four starts and a refitted nested boundary were compared; this does not prove a global optimum.','', '## All four maturities','', '| Days | Single loss contribution | Two-speed loss contribution | Single wing IV MAE | Two-speed wing IV MAE |','|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|']
